WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
hello  i'm michelle i'm a volunteer at raley  windmill  today we're going to talk about the  exhibition that's on the second floor of  the mill  the flower sack was a lucky find  the thatcher at the dutch cottage  mentioned to a volunteer that he had a  flower sack from the  windmill in his barn and was happy to  donate it  several small donations paid for the  restoration  the sack would have been used to hold  grain  or flour and when full would have  weighed about 200 pounds  donations have paid largely for the  items that are in the exhibition  one of the donations came from the  estate of thelma passfield  thelma and her family had lived in a  house in the grounds of the windmill  her father was a carton pear driver  there were a pair of suffolk punches  some shy horses a rather grumpy  donkey and a lively pony  another donation has come from one of  our volunteers  james crabb was the last miller under  windsail at raley mill  he was miller from 1888 to around  the early 1900s he retired at an early  age due to being unable to carry out a  full working week  that would have been six days then this  may have been due to miller's cough  but we're not sure he died in 1917.  beside the photo of james crabb you can  see  a small seed drill this would have been  used  to sow seeds in maybe an allotment  a garden or something like that  in the photo of james crabb you can see  that he's holding a sack barrow  there's a full-size example which forms  part of the exhibition  looking forward to seeing you when we're  open again  we have lots to show you in this  exhibition

